WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:06.911
Picture a café that lets its regulars pay at the end of the month.

2
00:00:06.911 --> 00:00:12.079
By the till sits a box of cards, one per regular, with the amount owed in pencil.

3
00:00:12.267 --> 00:00:17.449
Sam buys a coffee, the owner finds Sam's card, and changes one number.

4
00:00:17.449 --> 00:00:18.189
It's fast.

5
00:00:18.367 --> 00:00:20.254
Now make that box a program.

6
00:00:20.254 --> 00:00:29.623
Kafka is a program that keeps logs of records for other programs to read, and here it hands our program every card payment, 3,000 a second.

7
00:00:30.267 --> 00:00:35.607
The program keeps 2 million balances in memory, and adds each payment as it comes.

8
00:00:35.800 --> 00:00:37.972
One night the computer restarts.

9
00:00:37.972 --> 00:00:40.891
Memory is wiped, and every balance is gone.

10
00:00:41.100 --> 00:00:45.995
To get them back, the program must read every payment again from the start.

11
00:00:45.995 --> 00:00:50.628
A week of payments is 1.8 billion records, about five hours of reading.

12
00:00:50.833 --> 00:00:52.524
The tests never caught this.

13
00:00:52.524 --> 00:00:54.698
Tests never restart halfway through.

14
00:00:54.698 --> 00:00:59.408
So where should the table live, so that a restart costs minutes and not hours?

15
00:00:59.767 --> 00:01:00.992
Back to the café.

16
00:01:00.992 --> 00:01:08.631
Each time the owner changes a card, she also writes the new total as a line in a notebook: Sam owes 12.50.

17
00:01:08.631 --> 00:01:11.947
And she keeps the notebook in the office safe.

18
00:01:12.133 --> 00:01:15.795
Lose the box, and she rebuilds every card from the notebook.

19
00:01:15.795 --> 00:01:17.992
She never touches the till receipts.

20
00:01:18.167 --> 00:01:18.866
Map it out.

21
00:01:18.866 --> 00:01:24.329
The box of cards is the table, kept on the program's own disk, right next to the code.

22
00:01:24.329 --> 00:01:28.777
The notebook is a second log in Kafka, with one line for every change.

23
00:01:28.777 --> 00:01:31.953
And the till receipts are the payments themselves.

24
00:01:32.200 --> 00:01:37.347
Kafka Streams, a library you add to your own program, builds exactly this.

25
00:01:37.347 --> 00:01:41.728
It keeps the box of cards in a state store, RocksDB by default.

26
00:01:41.933 --> 00:01:48.353
RocksDB is a small database that keeps a value under each key, in files on the local disk.

27
00:01:48.353 --> 00:01:50.992
A read or a write takes microseconds.

28
00:01:51.200 --> 00:01:56.000
Every write to the store also goes to a Kafka topic called the changelog.

29
00:01:56.000 --> 00:01:57.315
That's the notebook.

30
00:01:57.600 --> 00:02:01.764
Kafka splits the payments into separate logs called partitions.

31
00:02:01.764 --> 00:02:05.400
Let's follow just one, partition 0, with five payments.

32
00:02:05.600 --> 00:02:07.853
Offset 0: acct-1 pays in 100.00.

33
00:02:07.853 --> 00:02:11.867
There's no row yet, so it starts at 0 and becomes 100.00.

34
00:02:11.867 --> 00:02:17.289
The new value goes into the store, and the same line goes into the changelog.

35
00:02:17.467 --> 00:02:19.904
Offset 1: acct-2 pays in 50.00.

36
00:02:19.904 --> 00:02:23.048
A new key, so a new row, and a new line.

37
00:02:23.233 --> 00:02:25.445
Offset 2: acct-1 pays 30.00.

38
00:02:25.445 --> 00:02:29.158
Its row changes from 100.00 to 70.00, in place.

39
00:02:29.158 --> 00:02:35.477
But a log never changes a line, so the changelog adds acct-1 = 70.00 at the end.

40
00:02:35.667 --> 00:02:39.252
Offset 3: acct-1 pays in 5.00 and reaches 75.00.

41
00:02:39.252 --> 00:02:45.749
Then the program commits: it saves its place on payments as 4, the next record to read.

42
00:02:45.900 --> 00:02:47.377
Notice the two shapes.

43
00:02:47.377 --> 00:02:49.795
The store keeps one row per account.

44
00:02:49.795 --> 00:02:52.683
The changelog keeps every change, in order.

45
00:02:53.033 --> 00:02:54.336
Now, the bad night.

46
00:02:54.336 --> 00:02:57.420
The computer dies, and its disk goes with it.

47
00:02:57.633 --> 00:03:01.129
The work moves to another computer with an empty disk.

48
00:03:01.129 --> 00:03:04.042
Before it handles payment 4, what does it do?

49
00:03:04.042 --> 00:03:05.077
Pause and guess.

50
00:03:08.267 --> 00:03:17.105
It reads the changelog from the beginning into a fresh store, oldest first: 100.00, then 50.00, then 70.00, then 75.00.

51
00:03:17.667 --> 00:03:20.564
For each account, the last line wins.

52
00:03:20.564 --> 00:03:23.696
So acct-1 is 75.00, and acct-2 is 50.00.

53
00:03:23.867 --> 00:03:28.449
Only then does it carry on with the payments, from the saved place, 4.

54
00:03:28.449 --> 00:03:31.133
acct-2 pays 20.00 and goes down to 30.00.

55
00:03:31.300 --> 00:03:33.364
Nothing came from the old disk.

56
00:03:33.364 --> 00:03:37.758
The payments, the changelog and the saved place all live in Kafka.

57
00:03:37.758 --> 00:03:40.088
Four lines to read, not five hours.

58
00:03:40.367 --> 00:03:44.932
You might be thinking: the notebook only needs the latest line per account.

59
00:03:44.932 --> 00:03:47.063
Kafka can clean a log down to that.

60
00:03:47.063 --> 00:03:50.776
It's called compaction, and the changelog has it switched on.

61
00:03:51.000 --> 00:03:57.223
So the lesson's demo adds up 1,000 payments for 10 accounts, then deletes the local store.

62
00:03:57.223 --> 00:03:59.919
How many records does the restore read?

63
00:03:59.919 --> 00:04:01.025
Pause and guess.

64
00:04:04.267 --> 00:04:04.710
1,000.

65
00:04:04.710 --> 00:04:05.671
Every update.

66
00:04:05.671 --> 00:04:13.652
Compaction never touches the newest file of a log, the one still being written, and this changelog is young.

67
00:04:13.833 --> 00:04:14.369
Well...

68
00:04:14.369 --> 00:04:15.592
so much for ten.

69
00:04:15.592 --> 00:04:19.187
The balances came back right, just more slowly.

70
00:04:19.533 --> 00:04:21.990
So the wait grows with the store.

71
00:04:21.990 --> 00:04:28.763
50 GB, read at 100 MB a second, is more than 8 minutes, and that partition's payments wait.

72
00:04:28.967 --> 00:04:32.819
One setting shortens it: num.standby.replicas, set to 1.

73
00:04:32.819 --> 00:04:38.665
Another copy of the program keeps a warm store by reading the changelog all the time.

74
00:04:38.833 --> 00:04:43.304
That's a second café keeping its own box from a copy of the notebook.

75
00:04:43.304 --> 00:04:46.932
After a failure, only the last few lines need replaying.

76
00:04:47.133 --> 00:04:56.399
The café picture breaks at scale: one box for the owner, but here one slice of the table per partition, and thousands of lines a second.

77
00:04:56.633 --> 00:04:58.838
Here it is in Java and Kotlin.

78
00:04:58.838 --> 00:05:03.836
groupByKey and aggregate are the fold: start at 0, add each payment.

79
00:05:04.033 --> 00:05:08.513
Materialized.as names the store, and the changelog comes with it.

80
00:05:08.513 --> 00:05:10.788
One setting asks for the standby.

81
00:05:11.267 --> 00:05:19.145
So the moral of the story: keep the table next to the code for speed, and write every change to a log that outlives the disk.

82
00:05:19.367 --> 00:05:21.058
The box of cards is fast.

83
00:05:21.058 --> 00:05:23.156
The notebook is what you trust.

84
00:05:23.500 --> 00:05:24.634
One quick check.

85
00:05:24.634 --> 00:05:26.476
acct-1 pays in 10.00 more.

86
00:05:26.476 --> 00:05:30.798
What changes in the store, and what changes in the changelog?

87
00:05:30.798 --> 00:05:31.932
Pause and think.

88
00:05:35.167 --> 00:05:39.484
The store overwrites one row: acct-1 becomes 85.00.

89
00:05:39.484 --> 00:05:44.140
The changelog adds one line at the end: acct-1 = 85.00.
